## Migration: .NET Framework → .NET 8

### Problems in the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()`** — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ (SYSLIB0006)
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()`** — obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable for waking `WaitOne`
3. **`ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` without cancellation** — no cooperative way to stop the loop
4. **Race condition** — multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals; only one item dequeued per wake

### Pattern Classification

| Site | Pattern | Replacement |
|------|---------|-------------|
| `while(true)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call interruption | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | `cts.Cancel()` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `cts.Cancel()` + `Join` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — exit the loop
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _queue.Add(item);
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();            // wakes the blocking call cooperatively
        _thread?.Join(5000);      // wait for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation is the only option
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

### Why `BlockingCollection<T>`

The original code hand-rolled a producer/consumer with `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock`. `BlockingCollection<T>` (available since .NET 4, works on .NET 8) encapsulates all of that **and** accepts a `CancellationToken` to unblock the consumer — eliminating the need for `Thread.Interrupt`/`Abort` entirely.

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Expecting instant stop** | `Cancel()` only unblocks at the next checkpoint (`GetConsumingEnumerable` check). If `Handle()` is long-running, pass `_cts.Token` into it too. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Implement `IDisposable`. |
| **Fallback to `Thread.Abort`** | There is no fallback in .NET 8. If `Handle()` can hang indefinitely on uncooperative code, move it to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill`. |
| **Original race condition** | The old code could lose signals (multiple `Set()` calls before `Reset()`). `BlockingCollection` fixes this — every `Add` is consumed exactly once. |
| **`Thread.Interrupt` not a safe substitute** | It only wakes `WaitSleepJoin` state and throws a *different* exception (`ThreadInterruptedException`). Don't use it as a stopgap. |

### If You Must Keep Manual Threading (no `BlockingCollection`)

```csharp
private void RunLoop()
{
    var handles = new[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };
    while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles); // wakes on either signal OR cancellation
        if (_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested) break;

        _newEvent.Reset();
        lock (_lock)
        {
            while (_queue.Count > 0)   // drain all queued items
                Handle(_queue.Dequeue());
        }
    }
}
```

This uses `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` to replace both `Thread.Interrupt` and `Thread.Abort` with cooperative cancellation.
